+# Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian. Like the rest of the Non-Goals table, it was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, so whether it was ever decided stayed open.
+
+A request arrived on 2026-08-21 to narrow the veterinarian directory to one specialty. That request reaches NG-9, and a request never reopens a recorded row by itself. The owner decided the row at intake, stating: "Non-goal NG-9 is narrowed: free-text veterinarian search stays out of scope, but filtering the directory by an attribute it already shows is in."
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole.** Decline the request. Rejected by the owner: the directory already shows each veterinarian's specialties, so narrowing by one adds no new surface for the reader to learn.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely.** Open search across pets, visits, and veterinarians. Rejected: the row's rationale holds for free-text search, which repeats what owner search already demonstrates.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to free-text search** (chosen). Attribute filtering leaves the row; free-text search stays in it.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 declines free-text search for pets, visits, and veterinarians. It no longer declines narrowing a listing to one value of an attribute that listing already shows. The veterinarian directory may therefore be narrowed to a single specialty, on both its page and its machine-readable form.
+
+The narrowing is bounded by what the owner decided. Free-text veterinarian search, pet search, and visit search remain declined, and each needs its own recorded owner decision before any request proceeds against the row.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001 is admissible, and NG-9 no longer blocks it.
+- Narrowing a listing by an attribute it already displays is now the recorded boundary line, so a comparable request elsewhere is a scoping question rather than a non-goal breach.
+- Free-text veterinarian search still fails the boundary check. So does any search over pets or visits.
+- The machine-readable veterinarian directory is a supported surface again, decided at the same intake. REQ-VET-002 stays withdrawn and its id is never reused.
+- NG-9 leaves the open provenance consultation for the narrowed part only. Whether the free-text half was ever deliberately declined is still unknown.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row and the preamble that names it.
+- [REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001](../prd.md#req-vetsspecialtyfilter-001) — the capability the narrowing admits.
