+# Specialty-Narrowed Veterinarian Lookups Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`VetRepository`'s two unnarrowed reads are annotated `@Cacheable("vets")`. `CacheConfiguration` creates that cache through the JCache API with statistics enabled and nothing else. Caffeine backs it at runtime, so the cache has no size limit and no eviction policy.
+
+REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001 adds two narrowed reads whose first argument is a specialty name taken straight from the query string. Annotating them `@Cacheable("vets")` would put caller-supplied text into the key of an unevicted cache.
+
+The existing `findAll(Pageable)` key already derives from the `page` request parameter, so an integer-shaped key space is part of the baseline. A free-text key space is a different order of exposure, and this change would be the one introducing it.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the narrowed reads on `vets`.** One annotation, consistent with the siblings. Every distinct specialty string mints a permanent entry, and a caller chooses the strings.
+2. **Cache them on a second, bounded cache.** Requires a Caffeine-specific size and expiry configuration, replacing the vendor-neutral JCache setup for one read.
+3. **Leave the narrowed reads uncached** (chosen). The cached key space stays closed to what the application itself derives.
+
+## Decision
+
+The narrowed reads carry no `@Cacheable` annotation. The `vets` cache holds the unnarrowed directory only.
+
+Directory narrowing reaches the database on every request. The seeded directory is six veterinarians, and the demonstration serves one replica, so the read cost is not a concern the cache exists to solve.
+
+Caching a request-keyed read on an unevicted cache stays out of this codebase until the cache carries a size limit and an eviction policy. Bounding the `vets` cache is [an open question](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) that this decision does not settle.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The narrowed listing is always current, while the unnarrowed one can serve a stale page. The two surfaces can disagree until the cache is evicted, and no write path for veterinarians exists to cause that today.
+- A caller cannot grow heap by naming distinct specialties.
+- A reader sees two annotated methods beside two unannotated ones on the same interface. This ADR is the back-link that explains the split.
+- Should veterinarian data grow past a demonstration's size, the narrowed reads are the first place a bounded cache is needed.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — `VetRepository` and the cache's scope.
+- [system-design.md#threat-model](../system-design.md#threat-model) — the caller-supplied cache key row.
+
+## References
+
+- [Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md) — the vendor-neutral configuration style this decision keeps.
+- [Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope](2026-08-21-non-goal-vet-directory-filtering.md) — the product decision admitting the narrowing.
